WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.760
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:03.700
My life this week has been dictated by a natural phenomenon.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.120
As Montana has continued through an unseasonably hot and dry summer,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.280
my valley has been socked with smoke, sometimes enough that

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.320
experts advised me not to leave my house -

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:13.780
certainly not with an infant.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.660
And that got substantially worse when the nearest fire in Lolo National Forest

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.640
flared and burned more than 9,000 acres in one evening.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.420
It was beautiful, though.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.240
I was headed out to get dinner for Katherine and

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.240
I didn't come home for an hour because I kept stopping to film it.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.080
And then, of course, this Monday there was the eclipse.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.360
And one of the remarkable things about the eclipse is

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.400
that we knew exactly when it was going to happen

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.760
for decades in advance.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.520
We had enough lead time to get a bunch of eclipse glasses made and

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.340
sent to gas stations all over the country.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.540
People booked hotel rooms and braved terrible traffic, though not before

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.440
first making sure that the sun would be out in the place where they were going.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.780
And we trusted all of those things.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.360
I couldn't tell you how people figured out when eclipses were gonna show up before computers,

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.560
but they did it.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.980
I also don't know how scientists fight fires or predict them or

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.460
know to tell me when I shouldn't go outside to avoid damaging my lungs.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.280
But I do know that someone knows.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.420
Someone knows when the eclipse will happen.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.040
Someone knows when fires will probably get worse,

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.420
and they've had their work checked by other people who also know.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.080
I don't have the space in my head to figure all these things out on my own,

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.340
and - like - good thing.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.780
Because the story of human progress is not a story of every single person figuring out

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.520
every single thing for themselves.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.220
John, when telling people that I trust experts, I sometimes hear people respond that

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.100
I'm committing a logical fallacy: appealing to authority.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.160
And this is a fallacy.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.000
When making an argument, saying "but this expert says so" is not a good argument.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.520
But, I'm not actually making an appeal to one person.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.460
I'm making an appeal to a process that has had a good deal of success at

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.680
accurately explaining and predicting stuff.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.380
The expert is the proxy for the process.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.720
I'm making an appeal not just to the people who have figured something out

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.820
but to all of the other people who I know are gonna check their work.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:58.080
I'm making an appeal to statistics and logic and calculus and peer review.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.400
I'm making an appeal to science.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.120
And also, I'm often not making an argument.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.640
I'm not trying to convince people of what they should believe.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.160
I'm trying to explain that when it comes to things that I'm

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.660
not that interested in or capable of learning enough about,

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.300
I'm happy to accept scientific consensus because

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.960
it's gonna be a whole lot better than whatever "hunch" I happen to have.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.340
This isn't trusting that they're right.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.420
It's accepting that people who study things for a living have a far greater chance of being right than

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.740
someone who just feels like arguing with them.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.840
It's not an appeal to authority.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.820
It's like an appeal to sanity.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:37.300
I get worried about the current tendency of some to feel like they must confirm everything for themselves.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.840
That's individualism taken to maybe a fatal extreme.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.800
Human progress is a story of building on the work of others,

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.180
not every single person starting from scratch.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.520
I agree that trusting individuals because they've had the expert label applied to them can lead to trouble,

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.540
but one of the great achievements of our culture, of our society, is

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:01.380
the creation and refinement of robust systems for creating and identifying trustable expertise.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.220
Of course, those systems can always be refined and improved,

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.800
but those who think they can just be discarded

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:08.340
scare me.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.300
Watching these beautiful and terrible plumes of smoke flare above my town,

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.300
I felt very grateful to those who study these things for a living so I do not have to.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.320
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:21.980
Endscreen!

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.740
John, happy 40th birthday yesterday.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:26.420
We made you a bunch of turtle cakes.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:27.960
I hope that you liked them.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.680
There are so many turtle cakes!

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:36.380
Also, ROLF, the dumb party game that DFTBA games is making, its kickstarter is ending in just a few days,

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:39.620
so if you want to help that out and pledge to get a copy, there's a link in the description.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.060
Also, to everybody on the gulf coast of Texas and Louisiana,

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.600
hopefully won't be as bad as it looks like it might be,

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.380
but be safe. Make good decisions.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.220
Thinking about you.

